---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import RotateImage from '@/components/tools/image-edit/RotateImage.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="rotate-image">
  <RotateImage client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to rotate an image</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Drop in one or more photos.</li>
      <li>Use the <strong>90° left</strong>, <strong>90° right</strong> and <strong>180°</strong> buttons for quarter turns, or the angle slider and number box for any other angle, to a tenth of a degree.</li>
      <li>Pick the fill for the corners that open at odd angles: transparent, white, black or a colour you choose.</li>
      <li>Check the live preview, then press <strong>Rotate</strong> and download the images one by one or as a ZIP.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Why photos come out sideways</h2>
    <p>
      A phone or camera sensor is fixed in the body, so it always reads out pixels in the same orientation,
      however you hold it. The camera does not rotate the data; it writes an EXIF tag,
      <code>Orientation</code>, that records which way was up. Software that reads the tag shows the photo
      correctly. Software that ignores it shows the raw sensor readout. For this reason the same file can be
      upright in your gallery and rotated 90° in a web form, an email client or an old desktop viewer.
    </p>
    <p>
      When you rotate an image here, the orientation is written into the pixels. The exported file needs no
      tag and looks the same everywhere, which is what you want before you upload to a system you do not
      control.
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>EXIF orientation</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Fix</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>1</td><td>Normal</td><td>Nothing to do</td></tr>
          <tr><td>3</td><td>Rotated 180°</td><td>Rotate 180°</td></tr>
          <tr><td>6</td><td>Rotated 90° counter-clockwise</td><td>Rotate 90° right</td></tr>
          <tr><td>8</td><td>Rotated 90° clockwise</td><td>Rotate 90° left</td></tr>
          <tr><td>2, 4, 5, 7</td><td>Mirrored variants</td><td>Use <a href="/flip-image">flip</a> as well</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Right angles are lossless; odd angles are not</h2>
    <p>
      At 90, 180 and 270 degrees each source pixel lands exactly on a destination pixel, so the rotation only
      rearranges pixels and loses nothing. At any other angle the grids do not line up, so each output pixel is
      a weighted blend of its neighbours. Every image editor has this effect, and it softens the finest detail
      slightly. When you straighten a scan by a degree or two, the effect is invisible.
    </p>

    <h2>The corners, and what to do with them</h2>
    <p>
      A rectangle rotated by an arbitrary angle needs a larger bounding box with four empty triangles. This
      tool makes the canvas larger to fit, so no part of your image is cropped, and fills the triangles with
      your chosen background. For a new width W′ and height H′ from an original W × H at angle θ:
    </p>
    <p>
      <code>W′ = |W·cos θ| + |H·sin θ|</code><br />
      <code>H′ = |W·sin θ| + |H·cos θ|</code>
    </p>
    <p>
      Transparent corners need PNG or WebP output. JPEG has no alpha channel, so a transparent choice becomes
      white. To remove the corners, rotate first and then trim them with the <a href="/crop-image">crop
      tool</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Straightening a crooked scan or horizon</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Find a line in the image that must be level: a horizon, a door frame, the edge of a page.</li>
      <li>Change the angle by ±0.5° at a time and compare that line with the edge of the preview panel.</li>
      <li>When it is level, crop inwards to remove the filled corners. Expect to lose 2–5% of the frame for each degree of correction.</li>
    </ol>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
